{"name":"Flesh Fly","hex":"#894585","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flesh fly","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any fly of the family Sarcophagidae that feed on carrion."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flesh_fly"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flesh fly","blocks":[{"text":"Sarcophagidae are a family of flies commonly known as flesh flies. They differ from most flies in that they are ovoviviparous, opportunistically depositing hatched or hatching maggots instead of eggs on carrion, dung, decaying material, or open wounds of mammals, hence their common name. Some flesh fly larvae are internal parasites of other insects such as Orthoptera, and some, in particular the Miltogramminae, are kleptoparasites of solitary Hymenoptera. The adults mostly feed on fluids from animal bodies, nectar, sweet foods, fluids from animal waste and other organic substances. Juveniles need protein to develop and may be laid on carrion, dung or sweet plant foods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sarcophaga_Bercaea2.jpg/330px-Sarcophaga_Bercaea2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flesh_fly"},{"title":"Flesh fly (fly fishing)","blocks":[{"text":"Flesh Fly is a popular fly pattern used by rainbow trout anglers in Western Alaska. This particular fly fishing pattern is designed to mimic a chunk of rotten salmon flesh washing down stream. The pattern is usually tied using white or off white rabbit hair."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flesh_fly_(fly_fishing)"},{"title":"Flesh","blocks":[{"text":"Flesh is any aggregation of soft tissues of an organism. Various multicellular organisms have soft tissues that may be called \"flesh\". In mammals, including humans, flesh encompasses muscles, fats and other loose connective tissues, but sometimes excludes non-muscular organs and typically discarded parts. More generally, it may be considered the portions of the body that are soft and delicate. In a culinary context, consumable animal flesh is called meat, while processed visceral tissues are known as offal."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flesh"},{"title":"Fly","blocks":[{"text":"A fly is an insect of the order Diptera, named for the single pair of wings. The hindwings have evolved into halteres, organs that sense rotation, giving flies precise control of their flight. Diptera is a large order containing more than 150,000 species, including horse-flies, crane flies, hoverflies, mosquitoes and others."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Bessenbandzweefvlieg_Vrouwtje_%282%29.JPG/330px-Bessenbandzweefvlieg_Vrouwtje_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fly"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg/500px-Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg/1280px-Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg/500px-Nervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wing venation of Sarcophagidae","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANervation_des_Sarcophagidae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg/500px-Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg/1280px-Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg/500px-Sarcophaga_nodosa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sarcophaga nodosa feeding on decaying flesh","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASarcophaga_nodosa.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Sarcophagid_sal.jpg/500px-Sarcophagid_sal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Sarcophagid_sal.jpg/500px-Sarcophagid_sal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sarcophagid showing basally plumose arista","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASarcophagid_sal.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Diptera_Sarcophagidae_Miltogramminae_Craticulina_showing_arista_Kleptoparasite_of_Philanthus_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Diptera_Sarcophagidae_Miltogramminae_Craticulina_showing_arista_Kleptoparasite_of_Philanthus_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Diptera_Sarcophagidae_Miltogramminae_Craticulina_showing_arista_Kleptoparasite_of_Philanthus_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Diptera_Sarcophagidae_Miltogramminae_Craticulina_showing_arista_Kleptoparasite_of_Philanthus_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Craticulina is a genus of Miltogramminae. This species is a kleptoparasite of Philanthus. The arista is basally pubescent.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiptera_Sarcophagidae_Miltogramminae_Craticulina_showing_arista_Kleptoparasite_of_Philanthus_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Standing-rib-roast.jpg/120px-Standing-rib-roast.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikime